{
  "dom_id": "step-q350681",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "What is the sum of the series $\\,1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
